—

Safety: "nothing bad happens"
Liveness: "something good eventually happens

Synchronous means that the CPU will wait for the operation. 



# Intro
Memory access model is what prevents processes from indexing into another process' address space in user mode.



# Processes
Orphaned processes are those that outlive their parent.

`exec()` does not create a new process; it overlays (overwrites) the current process with a new program. It does not return, unless the system call failed and the rest of the code would run.

`wait()` blocks the caller process. Zombie processes are child processes that have terminated, but the parent hasn't called `wait()`. So the associated PCB is left allocated (but mostly everything else is deallocated). Calling `wait()` when there are zombie processes returns one of the PIDs immediately and deallocates the PCB. `exit()` cleans up all waiting zombies.

`kill()` can be used for interprocess communication. The receiving process can have signal handlers. 



# Threads
<!-- TODO: scheduler handles processes and kernel threads? schedules in units of threads? and i thought there was time-sharing? -->
Threads have the same life cycle as processes, and when one thread blocks, another thread gets to run. There's no time-sharing, though. However, running threads can call a function to yield themselves "manually" and make way for another thread.

Condition variable functions take in the lock.

The semantics are about which thread should get the lock after `signal()` is called.



# Booting
1. CPU loads boot program (e.g., UEFI)
2. Boot program puts hardware information into a data structure
3. Boot program loads the OS' kernel and gives it the hardware data structure
4. OS initializes kernel data structures (e.g., ready queue) and hardware devices (to ready) and starts basic processes
5. OS will run user programs (double-click)
   - If there aren't any, enter the idle loop and do some system management and profiling tasks. Might halt the processor and enter low-power mode after some time, waking up when a hardware interrupt is received



# Memory
Local page replacement algorithm only consider the pages owned by the process. This requires a fixed number of frames per process. Global page replacement algorithms consider all the pages. This allows processes to use up as many frames as they need. 

- Small pages: reduce internal fragmentation
- Large pages: reduce number of page table entries, page faults, and I/O time

- Immediately respond to request
- 8-byte alignment
- Handle any sequence of requests
- Only use space of the heap



# Storage
`link()` just adds another entry to an existing file. Hard link. Basically an alternate name. The file has link count. If `unlink()` brings the link count to zero, the file is deleted.

`fsync()` does not return until all the all data is written and flushes the buffer cache. Actually, not really. Maybe.

SSDs have a no-op scheduler—serve requests in the order they come in. This is not FIFO because it doesn't guarantee it performs the requests in the order they're generated (too much work).

OS checks permissions as inodes come in.



# Networks
Parallel programming has two paradigms: shared memory (threads) and message passing (connections). In shared memory, communication is very fast and implicit. However, synchronization is explicit. The processes and threads must also be executing on the same computer.

mb -> b, s -> μs



# Parallel and Distributed
Since parallel systems have multiple CPUs, each CPU does have their own caches (plus a shared one).

Symmetric Multi Processor (SMP) is a system with a motherboard of multiple CPUs.

Virtualization allows multiple OSs to run on the same machine at the same time. This is through the hypervisor, that intercept and handle hardware requests from the OS.

Happened-Before Relation (letters are events):
- A and B are of the same process and A executed first, A → B
- A is a message send and B is a message receive, A → B
- A → B and B → C, A → C



# Security
There are five types of rootkits depending on where they hide: firmware, hypervisor, kernel, library, application.

Steganography is hiding a message in plain sight (e.g., an image). 